\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part Ten already got an introduction.
 A different way of thinking is about what is going on here is that this is a form of life–logging.
 Or,
 in 18th century terms,
 a diary.
 Just not anywhere near as compelling as those written by the famous diarists.
 This one is more of a mental self–portrait.
 And not even for you,
 but for myself.
 Not to cast a narcissistic gaze at my own words,
 but to organize my own thoughts.
 Still in the experimental stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
20 Sept 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I just scanned what I wrote in Part Ten-J,
 and it feels like I've already forgotten half of what I wrote there.
 Does that mean that I record unmemorable,
 unimportant things in my diary?
 Well,
 I'll remember very little of this many months,
 many years hence,
 so not important enough to place into decadal memory.
 And yet,
 it was important when I was thinking of it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So there's this spiritual process of living.
 If it's partying down,
 it can be partly recording with photos and video.
 If it's intellectual theorizing,
 the high points can be written in a diary.
 I ingress not only into my brain,
 but also into a slightly more permanent medium:
 this text.
 Hmm.
 I'm shitting my thoughts into physical reality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I need to make a poster for Ruta's party.
 I will try to place ingress into it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I need to figure out how to use one of those AI drawing systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
21 Sept 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I just wrote this in a private email:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I daydream of living in a commune.
 While I was in Hong Kong,
 David Hanson put everyone up in these apartments;
 there'd be like four of us living together.
 It was fun,
 we'd come home,
 and it was like a party after work,
 every day.
 But this lasted only a few months,
 before I went home.
 Would it wear thin after a while?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Coed.
 A girl lived with us (three men).
 Audrey.
 And somehow I would end up spending more time with her.
 Where were the other guys?
 I don't remember.
 Traveling?
 We just got very comfortable with each other's presence,
 laughs,
 goofing off,
 but also opening up with things that hurt when we were younger.
 Psychological intimacy.
 I really liked her,
 it was a developing relationship.  I think I just really really like being around women.  Much much more than being around guys.
 And I'm not a womanizer;
 I don't chase;
 I just honestly enjoy it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So now I think about what it would be like if I lived in a commune.
 There'd be women around,
 and it would be a matter of not very much time before I started wrapping my heart around one of them.
 This creates issues,
 the main one being I'm just not young any more.
 Trouble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is how I got into trouble with Milda.
 All she had to do was smile at me.
 And living together,
 as housemates,
 contact every day,
 does the rest.
 It's magical,
 I like it,
 but it's like a drug addiction,
 I suffer until I can get some more and feel high again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
– Linas
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
p.s.
 to post–rationalize a bit:
 part of the study of AGI has to be the emotional dimension,
 and the spiritual dimension,
 so I'm deep–diving into what "it actually feels like",
 instead of being just clinical and academic.
 The only problem is that it feels like heroin,
 or meth,
 or whatever.
 I have not figured out how to connect this back up to AGI.  Other than to say that,
 for example,
 bacteria would have to subjectively feel hunger,
 and so already,
 there's a roller coaster of the pain of being hungry and the satisfaction of being full. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It's important,
 because the foundation of "don't kill us all" safety ethics for AGI has to be respect for life,
 and,
 in mammals,
 that foundation is provided by (mother–child) love. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whatever.
 I write too much.
\end_layout

